lines=['# Movement Lab 01C — Controlled Transitions','', f"**Bounded pilot: {len(audit)} validated ten-second trials. Flexion established a stable changed configuration; opposite flexion and signed head yaw showed partial return. No exact return, symmetric crouch, meaningful height extension, unloading or step was established. Coverage omitted by the launch cap: {', '.join(missing) if missing else 'none'}.**", '', '[Compact atlas and synchronized recorded-state replay](comparison.html) · [Independent validation](independent-validation.json) · [Per-joint command/response analysis](joint-analysis.json)', '', '## Evidence and smallest credible experiment','', '01B used the existing checkpoint-99 standing actor with additive joint-position residuals. All 14 scalar position targets are addressable through the action manager; this is not independent control of actual joint angles, body pose, force, or COM. HOME + actor + residual remains the action semantics, with unit scale, unmodified observation pipeline and checkpoint normalizer. BAM retains four physics steps of delay, voltage/sag, friction and torque constraints. Feedback can oppose commands; modeled limits do not guarantee tracking.', '', 'The 01B flex command did not produce a symmetric crouch: one knee barely moved and the principal displacement was forward. After the residual ended, actual offsets and actor targets remained altered. The final left-ankle offset was −0.01353 rad while its actor-target difference was +0.01745 rad; the final COM difference was predominantly forward (1.511 mm). This is evidence of a persistent closed-loop/plant state, not a still-active residual. Friction, compliance and controller adaptation cannot be separated causally without a further intervention.', '', 'Positive head yaw in 01B fell from a 0.04330 rad peak to 0.02065 rad at six seconds. Late actor-target difference was −0.01381 rad, and the actual joint was moving only about −0.000285 rad/s. Negative yaw retained −0.00495 rad. The late near-plateau favors controller/plant persistence over a fast return cut off by the evaluation window, but the old window alone cannot rule out slow recovery. This pilot changes the hold/return timing and observes longer; it cannot independently isolate timing from controller behavior. [Recomputed 01B windows](../evidence01b.json).', '', 'The 1 mm sole-clearance reset remains appropriate for this nominal controlled study: it reduces the unsupported landing transient without pinning the free root. Root reset z remains 0.1181823627 m, HOME joints and zero generalized velocity. Curated groundcontact, solver and unchanged **3 mm penetration limit** remain authoritative. It is not evidence that this initialization is optimal or robust.', '', '## Protocol and interpretation','', 'Preregistered bounded pilot: baseline, signed flex/extension, signed head yaw, signed sagittal rocking, final zero repeat. Eight ten-second trials, one seed (42), at most 80 simulated seconds. The driver stops before movements if the baseline or matched-history audit fails and stops launching after 510 wall seconds, reserving 90 seconds for the last trial. No training. Amplitude signs and command families form a small interpretable exploration; no scalar reward or displacement optimization and no outcome replacement. CLI exposes amplitude scale, rise, hold and return duration; the pilot fixes their magnitudes rather than running a factorial timing sweep.', '', 'Every pulse starts at 2 s, rises over 0.6 s, holds 1.0 s, and returns over 1.4 s; residual is zero from 5 s through 10 s. This extends the original 01B return and observation windows. Head peaks are ±0.08 rad. Flex peaks are hip pitches L/R −/+0.03, knees +/−0.05, ankles −/+0.02 rad; extension reverses them. Rock peaks are hip pitches L/R −/+0.02 and ankles +/−0.02 rad; reversing signs probes the opposite direction. Names describe commands, not guaranteed achieved motion. Asymmetric leg trials were omitted because 01B showed no threshold-level response or unloading.', '', 'The same independent physical gate checks the whole trajectory. Pre-command [1.5,2) s and final [9.5,10] s use 01B stability criteria: both feet supported, no nonfoot contact, tilt below 12°, COM speed ≤0.03 m/s, root angular speed ≤0.3 rad/s and horizontal per-axis COM range ≤5 mm. Late hold uses its final half-second. Actual matched-control response must reach 0.005 rad at a joint or 0.5 mm COM distance during the pulse. A stable changed configuration is an accepted transition result even if its offset persists. Partial return separately requires each detected response channel to contract below half its peak. Absolute final return additionally applies unchanged 01B pose tolerances; it is not exact recovery. Quiet survival alone is never evidence of return or an achieved movement.', '', '## Achieved configurations and transitions','', '| Command | Peak / final joint response (rad) | Peak / final COM response (mm) | Hold / final stable | Outcome |','|---|---:|---:|---|---|']
for n,r in audit.items():
 lines.append(f"| {n} | {r['peak_joint_rad']:.5f} / {r['final_joint_rad']:.5f} | {r['peak_com_mm']:.3f} / {r['final_com_mm']:.3f} | {r['stable_hold']} / {r['stable_final']} | {r['classification']} |")
